*  charcode.sno -- a character to its code and back again.
*  PENDING: ALPHA is never filled in, so &ALPHABET reads as 256 blanks
*  and neither of these functions can work. S4D58 3.6 says MDATA holds
*  "ALPHA, a string that consists of all characters arranged in the
*  order of their internal numerical representation", and
*  pkg/sil/copyseg/mdata.sil declares it with BUFFER, which 6.17 says
*  assembles blanks. AMPST, COLSTR and QTSTR are declared the same way
*  and have the same problem. Clear this line when they are filled.
*  Part of github.com/mdhender/sil. BSD 2-Clause; see LICENSE.
*
*  Version 3.11 has no CHAR or ORD, and should not need them:
*  &ALPHABET is every character of the host set in collating order, so
*  a code is a position in it. TAB(N) LEN(1) reads one out; BREAK(C)
*  with the cursor operator puts one back. What this prints is
*  therefore a fact about the host character set, which is ASCII here.
        &ANCHOR = 1
        DEFINE('CHAR(N)')                        :(CHAR.END)
CHAR    &ALPHABET TAB(N) LEN(1) . CHAR           :S(RETURN)F(FRETURN)
CHAR.END
        DEFINE('ORD(C)')                         :(ORD.END)
ORD     &ALPHABET BREAK(C) @ORD                  :S(RETURN)F(FRETURN)
ORD.END
*
        OUTPUT = 'A is ' ORD('A')
        OUTPUT = 'a is ' ORD('a')
        OUTPUT = '0 is ' ORD('0')
        OUTPUT = '65 is ' CHAR(65)
        OUTPUT = '97 is ' CHAR(97)
        OUTPUT = 'round trip: ' CHAR(ORD('Z'))
END
